        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Prague Spring</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Czechoslovak Socialist Republic, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a> and Wenceslas Square; the talks were held at Čierna nad Tisou and Bratislava, and Dubček was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> after the invasion.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Slow de-Stalinisation under Antonín Novotný (late 1950s and 1960s); Dubček became First Secretary on 5 January 1968; the Action Programme of April 1968; talks at Čierna nad Tisou (29 July to 1 August) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a> Declaration (3 August); invasion on the night of 20–21 August 1968; normalisation from April 1969.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>, First Secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludvik-svoboda">Ludvík Svoboda</a>, who became President in March; the reformers Josef Smrkovský, Oldřich Černík and František Kriegel; the hardliners Vasil Biľak, Drahomír Kolder and Oldřich Švestka; Eduard Goldstücker, head of the Writers' Union; the economist Ota Šik; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a>, who replaced Dubček in 1969.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion ended the reforms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a> reversed almost all of them in a period of normalisation, and only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">federalisation</a> of the country into Czech and Slovak republics survived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> remained a Soviet satellite state until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a> of 1989.</span></div>
        </div>
