    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s Action Programme of April 1968 promised freedom of the press, speech and movement, a partial decentralisation of the economy and a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, under the continued leadership of the Communist Party, in what he called &quot;socialism with a human face&quot;.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> tried negotiations at Čierna nad Tisou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, and then, on the night of 20–21 August 1968, troops from the Soviet Union, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> occupied the country; Dubček called on his people not to resist, and the resistance that followed was largely nonviolent.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a>, normalisation reversed almost all of the reforms from 1969; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">federalisation</a> of the country was the only formal change to survive, and the memory of 1968 stayed contested for decades.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>By August 1968 the censor-free journal Literární listy had a circulation of 300,000, making it the most published periodical in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

